{"findings":[{"rule_id":"NM-002","evidence":[{"kind":"note","ref":"N1","page":1,"quote":"INDUCTION HARDENED TO BE DONE FOR INDICATED AREA"},{"kind":"note","ref":"N3","page":1,"quote":"HEAT TREATMENT NOTE:- 1) PROFILE CUTTING SHOULD BE DONE. 2) TO BE CASE CARBURISING DONE. 2) TO BE INDUCTION HARDENED UPTO 50 ~ 55 HRC. 3) CASE DEPTH 0.8~ 1.2 mm."}],"comment":"Note N1 states induction hardening is to be done only on an 'indicated area', but note N3 lists induction hardening as a general heat-treatment step without limiting it to any area, and no dimension or view marks where that area is. This contradiction leaves the heat-treat shop unable to determine which surfaces require induction hardening versus carburising alone; the designer should reconcile N1 and N3 so both specify the same scope or clearly identify the indicated area."
    ,
    "recommended_correction": "Revise N1 or N3 so they agree on whether induction hardening applies to the whole part or only a specific area, and if specific, add a clear indication (hatching or a dimension) on the drawing showing that area."}]}
